      "good": "Team names wrap at spaces into consistent two-line rows, making each record easy to follow. Headers and signed goal differences stay intact, and the statistical columns have a regular rhythm.",
      "problems": "Every team occupies two lines, yet the extra team-column space does not shorten any row. The initial viewport cuts into GD and leaves Pts offscreen, so readers must scroll to see two important standings measures.",
      "cause": "The 120px team column provides 99px for text: enough to preserve individual words, but not enough to keep any full team name on one line. The total width is 453px."
    },
    {
      "design": "r2B",
      "good": "The 108px team column still preserves whole words and the same consistent two-line rows as r2A. Its narrower footprint brings more of GD into the initial viewport and reduces the scroll distance.",
      "problems": "GD is still cut by the viewport edge and Pts is offscreen. All seven names still take two lines, creating a tall table for a short set of standings.",
      "cause": "The team column provides 87px of text space, just enough for the longest unbreakable word. The statistical columns require a further 332px, giving a 441px rendered table."
    }
  ],
